/* ==============================================================
   06 — Motion settings for linear moves
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetCartLinVel, SetCartAngVel, SetCartAcc
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Speed in mm/s — a number the customer already knows
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   REAL UNITS

   Joint moves are tuned in percentages. Cartesian moves are
   tuned in millimetres per second:

       SetCartLinVel(v)    tool tip linear velocity, mm/s
                           (0.001 to 5000; default 150)
       SetCartAngVel(w)    tool turning velocity, deg/s
                           (0.001 to 5000; default 45)
       SetCartAcc(p)       Cartesian acceleration, % of max
                           (default 50)

   This matters more than it sounds. Process speeds are specified
   in real units: a dispensing spec says 25 mm/s, a wipe test
   says 100 mm/s, an insertion says 5 mm/s. With SetCartLinVel
   you type the number from the spec. There is no calibration
   step and no conversion to get wrong.

   These settings affect ONLY the linear commands — MoveLin,
   MoveLinRelTrf, MoveLinRelWrf. They have no effect on
   MoveJoints, MovePose or MoveJump, which are joint-space moves
   and obey SetJointVel. A customer who sets SetCartLinVel and
   sees nothing change is almost always using MovePose.

   TWO VELOCITIES, ONE MOVE

   A linear move can translate AND turn the tool at the same
   time. Those are governed separately: SetCartLinVel for the
   travel, SetCartAngVel for the turning. The robot respects
   both, so whichever is the binding constraint sets the pace.
   If a move seems inexplicably slow, and the tool is turning a
   long way while barely translating, SetCartAngVel is the one
   holding it back.

   That one bites on the MCS500 more than you would expect. The
   default of 45 deg/s is modest, and turning a part through 90
   degrees while moving it is an everyday SCARA job: at the
   default, that turn alone takes two seconds, whatever the
   linear speed says.

   THE CEILING STILL APPLIES

   You can ask for 5000 mm/s and not get it. The actual tip
   velocity may be lower — never higher — than requested,
   because the joint velocity limits still apply underneath. Near
   the arm's full stretch, a modest tip speed can demand enormous
   joint speed, and the robot will slow the tip to stay within
   limits rather than exceed them. (03_advanced/07 shows this.)

   A NOTE ON SetCartAcc

   Using SetCartAcc brings the robot to a complete stop between
   moves even when blending is enabled. If you are chasing cycle
   time with blending (02_intermediate/06), be aware that this
   command works against it. And although it accepts values up
   to 600 for compatibility with older programs, the manual says
   values above 100 give no practical benefit on this robot and
   may deform the path. Stay at or below 100.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)

// --- Get into position ----------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(170, -70, 80, 0)
Delay(1)


// --- Process speed: 10 mm/s -----------------------------------
// Insertion, probing, delicate placement. Deliberate and slow.
SetCartLinVel(10)
MoveLin(170, 70, 80, 0)
MoveLin(170, -70, 80, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Process speed: 100 mm/s ----------------------------------
// A typical dispensing or inspection pass. Same two lines.
SetCartLinVel(100)
MoveLin(170, 70, 80, 0)
MoveLin(170, -70, 80, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Transfer speed: 500 mm/s ---------------------------------
// Moving between stations, where the line is required but the
// speed is not part of the process.
SetCartLinVel(500)
MoveLin(170, 70, 80, 0)
MoveLin(170, -70, 80, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Translating and turning together -------------------------
// Same 140 mm of travel, but the tool also turns 90 degrees
// along the way — a part being squared up as it is carried. At
// 200 mm/s the travel alone would take 0.7 s; the turn, at the
// 15 deg/s set here, takes six. Watch the angular limit govern.
SetCartLinVel(200)
SetCartAngVel(15)                       // deliberately restrictive
MoveLin(170, 70, 80, 90)

// Now allow the turn to keep up. Same move back, same linear
// velocity, and it completes far faster.
SetCartAngVel(180)
MoveLin(170, -70, 80, 0)


// --- Back to defaults and home --------------------------------
SetCartLinVel(150)
SetCartAngVel(45)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Get the speed your process runs at, then type it into
      SetCartLinVel and run the pass. Watch your own number
      execute.

   2. Set SetCartLinVel(1000) and run a pass. It will not be ten
      times faster than the 100 mm/s pass over this short stroke
      — acceleration and the joint limits are governing, not
      your requested velocity.

   3. Delete the SetCartAngVel(15) line and run the turning pass
      at the firmware default of 45 deg/s. Two seconds for the
      turn. That is the number to remember the first time a
      customer's "simple" carry-and-rotate looks sluggish.

   NEXT   07_delay_and_sequencing.mxprog — how the queue really
          executes, and how to synchronise with the outside world.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
